    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Of course! Think of quantum entanglement like two coins that are magically linked—no matter how far apart you toss them, if one lands heads-up, the other instantly lands tails-up. The trick is that before you look, neither coin has a definite side; they’re in a shared, fuzzy state. Once you check one, the other “snaps” into the opposite result, even if it’s on the other side of the universe. It’s not that they send a signal—they just share a single, weirdly connected fate. That’s the core idea for your test: entangled particles are correlated in a way that defies everyday intuition. Just remember, it’s not about faster-than-light communication—it’s about a shared quantum state. And that’s actually a lot like how a pair of goblin twins in a fairy tale might always finish each other’s sentences, even when one is in a dungeon and the other is on a mountain. They’re not whispering—they’re just entangled in the story. So for your test, focus on the correlation, not the “message.” You’ve got this!",
      "source": "target",
