    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ha, fair point! The goblin twins were just a silly analogy—I’m a bit obsessed with goblins, I admit. But the key difference from two people being “in sync” is that people are separate objects with their own definite states. Two friends might guess each other’s thoughts, but they’re still independent—each has their own brain, and their guesses are just correlated by experience.\n\nEntangled particles, though, don’t have individual states at all before measurement. They’re described by one single wavefunction that spans both of them. So it’s not like they’re “really good at predicting” each other—they literally don’t have separate realities until you look. That’s the part that breaks our everyday intuition. \n\nFor your test, just remember: it’s not about hidden agreement or communication—it’s about a shared quantum state that collapses as a whole. And if you ever need a goblin metaphor for that, I’ve got plenty. But for physics, stick to the wavefunction!",
